Windows x64 native whole-file operations

windows-files-author.zero contains original instruction data and resolves its
own relative branches in Zero. Its output is windows-files.zero.part.
The compiler incorporates that table and windows-imports.zero.part directly.
No assembler, C runtime, shell or child process is used by these operations.
They call Windows KERNEL32 APIs through the generated PE import table.

create preserves an existing regular file, or creates an empty regular file.
del removes an existing regular file. Both return 0 on success and -1 on error.
readfile reads the complete file, with a capacity check and an end-of-file check.
save creates or replaces a file; writefile requires a previously absent file.
The three whole-file operations return a byte count, or -1 on error. Writes are
flushed before success. Replacement is not transactional; an I/O error can leave
a partial replacement. A failed exclusive write attempts to remove its new file.

watch returns regular-file names in a literal directory. search splits its
path at the last separator and matches the last component with an original
case-sensitive glob: * matches a sequence, ? matches one UTF-8 scalar.
Search without wildcards is an exact basename match. Empty watch/search paths
use the current directory. An empty trailing search component means all files.
Results are UTF-8 basenames, each followed by NUL; the result is the complete
byte count. Empty results return 0. Enumeration order is native and unsorted.

go changes the current process directory. disks returns NUL-separated native
drive roots such as C:\. It uses GetLogicalDrives; if that API returns zero,
it probes roots A:\ through Z:\ with GetFileAttributesW. A completely missing
drive set returns -1. Public indices remain a language-level concern.

Paths have a limit of 4095 UTF-8 bytes. Buffers have a limit of 65536 bytes.
Any overflow or short read fails the whole operation; callers must ignore the
buffer on failure. A zero capacity succeeds only for empty data/results.
Whole-file operations and directory selection reject a final reparse point.
These checks do not provide a sandbox against changing ancestor directories.

The tests exercise generated PE files under Wine on Linux. This establishes
Windows API/ABI compatibility in Wine; it does not claim a native Windows run.
windows-probe.zero is the operation probe. windows-bounds.zero tests null
arguments, the 65536-byte boundary, exact binary round trips, exclusive-create
preservation, and the 4095/4096-byte path boundary. External fixture orchestration
and Wine itself reside outside the workspace.
